          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The notion of
            %strong
              final
              = succeed "-functor" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            (also called a
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                cofinal
                = succeed "-functor" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn ∞
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CofinalTerminologyWarning"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertical+categorification"} vertical categorification
            of the notion of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/final+functor"} final functor
            from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            to
            = succeed "-theory:" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
          %p
            An
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functor"} (∞,1)-functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi K
                  %mo ′
                  %mo →
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \colon K' \to K
            is final precisely if precomposition with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preserved+colimit"} preserves
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-colimits"} (∞,1)-colimits
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            is final then for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi K
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \colon K \to C
            any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functor"} (∞,1)-functor
            we have
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mi lim
                    %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi K
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi F
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %munder
                    %mi lim
                    %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi K
                  %mo ′
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi p
                  %mi K
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi F
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\lim_\to (K \stackrel{F}{\to} C)
                  \\simeq
                  \\lim_{\to} ( K' \stackrel{p}{\to} K \stackrel{F}{\to} C),
          %p
            when either of these
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-colimits"} (∞,1)-colimits
            exist.
          %h2#Terminology Terminology
          %p The terminology conventions surrounding the topic of final functors are a bit of a mess. Often
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                “final map” is used to refer to
                = succeed "-functors" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-colimit"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                  \-colimiting
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocones"} cocones
            %li
              %p
                “initial map” is used for functors for which restriction preserves
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-limit"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                  \-limiting
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cones"} cones
          %p
            A mnemonic rule for this terminology is that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/final+objects"} final objects
            are picked out by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/final+functors"} final functors
            and likewise for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+objects"} initial objects
          %p
            But beware that this convention is not universal, for instance
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lurie"} Lurie
          %p
          #CofinalTerminologyWarning.num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              Beware that the term “cofinal (∞,1)-functor” can mean either a functor for which the precomposition functor preserves colimits (as in Lurie’s
              = succeed ")" do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Topos+Theory"} Higher Topos Theory
              = succeed ")." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Categories+and+Homotopical+Algebra"} Higher Categories and Homotopical Algebra
            %p
              Given that the two main sources for
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasicategories"} quasicategories
              assign opposite meanings to this term, it is best to avoid its usage altogether.
            %p
              Further adding to the confusion is that some sources, like
              = succeed "\u2018s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Francis+Borceux"} Borceux
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Handbook+of+Categorical+Algebra"} Handbook of Categorical Algebra
              use the term “final functor” for a functor for which the precomposition functor preserves limits, in contrast to the majority of the literature. Such usage, fortunately, is marginal.
            %p In more detail:
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Joyal"}> Joyal
                  ‘s
                  = succeed ":" do
                    %em
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/The+Theory+of+Quasi-Categories+and+its+Applications"} The Theory of Quasi-Categories and its Applications
                %p
                  colimit-preserving:
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %em final
                %p
                  limit-preserving:
                  %em initial
                  (page 171);
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cisinski"}> Cisinski
                  ‘s
                  = succeed ":" do
                    %em
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Categories+and+Homotopical+Algebra"} Higher Categories and Homotopical Algebra
                %p
                  colimit-preserving:
                  %em final
                  (Definition .4.1.8);
                %p
                  limit-preserving:
                  %em cofinal
                  (Definition 4.4.13);
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riehl"}> Riehl
                  –
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Verity"}> Verity
                  ‘s
                  = succeed ":" do
                    %em
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Elements+of+%E2%88%9E-Category+Theory"} Elements of ∞-Category Theory
                %p
                  colimit-preserving:
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %em final
                %p
                  limit-preserving:
                  %em initial
                  (Definition 2.4.5).
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lurie"}> Lurie
                  ‘s
                  = succeed ":" do
                    %em
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Topos+Theory"} Higher Topos Theory
                %p
                  colimit-preserving:
                  %em cofinal
                  (Definition 4.1.1.1);
                %p limit-preserving: (no terminology introduced);
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lurie"}> Lurie
                  ‘s
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Algebra"} Higher Algebra
                  (page 13) and
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Spectral+Algebraic+Geometry"} Spectral Algebraic Geometry
                  (Section 0.5, page 60):
                %p
                  colimit-preserving:
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %em left cofinal
                %p
                  limit-preserving:
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %em right cofinal
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lurie"}> Lurie
                  ‘s
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kerodon"} Kerodon
                  (
                  %a{:href => "https://kerodon.net/tag/02N1"}> Tag 02N1
                  ):
                %p
                  colimit-preserving:
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %em right cofinal
                %p
                  limit-preserving (
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functors"}> initial (∞,1)-functors
                  ):
                  = succeed "." do
                    %em left cofinal
            %p
              See also the warning in the article
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/final+functor"} final functor
              (
              %a{:href => "final+functor#WarningOnTerminology"}> here
              ).
          %h2#definition Definition
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              %strong (final morphism of simplicial set)
            %p
              A morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo :
                    %mi S
                    %mo →
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p : S \to T
              of
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
              %strong final
              if for every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrations+of+quasi-categories"} right fibration
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to T
              the induced morphism of simplicial sets
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi sSet
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mo ,
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi sSet
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo ,
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    sSet_{/T}(T,X) \to sSet_{/T}(S,X)
            %p
              is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+equivalence"} homotopy equivalence
          %p
            So in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/overcategory"} overcategory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi sSet
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sSet/T
            a final morphism is an object such that morphisms out of it into any right fibration are the same as morphisms out of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
            into that right fibration.
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo {
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi T
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mrow
                              %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                %mo =
                            %mo ↘
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mo ↙
                              %mrow
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi T
                    %mo }
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≃
                  %mrow
                    %mo {
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi S
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mrow
                              %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                %mi p
                            %mo ↘
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mo ↙
                              %mrow
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi T
                    %mo }
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\left\{
                  \\array{
                  T &amp;&amp;\to&amp;&amp; X
                  \\\
                  \&amp; {}_{\mathllap{=}}\searrow &amp;&amp; \swarrow_{}
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; T
                  }
                  \\right\}
                  \\;\;
                  \\simeq
                  \\left\{
                  \\array{
                  S &amp;&amp;\to&amp;&amp; X
                  \\\
                  \&amp; {}_{\mathllap{p}}\searrow &amp;&amp; \swarrow_{}
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; T
                  }
                  \\right\}
                  \\,.
          %p
            This definition is originally due to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andre+Joyal"} Andre Joyal
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Topos+Theory"} HTT, def 4.1.1.1
          %p
            This is equivalent to the following definition, in terms of the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+for+right+fibrations"} model structure for right fibrations
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              The morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo :
                    %mi S
                    %mo →
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p : S \to T
              is final precisely if the terminal morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo →
                    %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mrow
                        %mtable
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mi S
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %mo →
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %mi T
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %msub
                                %mrow
                                %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                  %mrow
                              %mo ↘
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %msub
                                %mo ↙
                                %mo =
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %mi T
                      %mo )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (p \to *) =   \left(
                    \\array{
                    S &amp;&amp;\to&amp;&amp; T
                    \\\
                    \&amp; {}_{\mathllap{}}\searrow &amp;&amp; \swarrow_{=}
                    \\\
                    \&amp;&amp; T
                    }
                    \\right)
              in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/overcategory"} overcategory
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi sSet
                      %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sSet_T
              is a weak equivalence in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+for+right+fibrations"} model structure for right fibrations
              on
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi sSet
                        %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sSet_T
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              This is
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Topos+Theory"} HTT, prop. 4.1.2.5
          .num_cor
            %h6#corollary Corollary
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complex"} Kan complex
              then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo :
                    %mi S
                    %mo →
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p : S \to T
              is final precisely if it is a weak equivalence in the standard
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+simplicial+sets"} model structure on simplicial sets
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              This is
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Topos+Theory"} HTT, cor. 4.1.2.6
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              Suppose
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : C \to D
              is a functor between ordinary categories. If the induced morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N(F) : N(C) \to N(D)
              is a final (∞,1)-functor, then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/final+functor"} final functor
              in the sense of ordinary category theory.
            %p
              However, the converse need not be true. For example, while the inclusion of the parallel pair
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ⇉
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [1] \rightrightarrows [0]
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta^{op}
              is final in the sense of ordinary category theory, it is not a final (∞,1)-functor.
          %h2#Properties Properties
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              %strong (preservation of undercategories and colimits)
            %p
              A morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo :
                    %mi K
                    %mo ′
                    %mo →
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p : K' \to K
              of
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasicategory"} quasicategory
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %ul
              %li
                and for every morphism
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mi F
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                      %mo :
                      %msup
                        %mi K
                        %mo ▹
                      %mo →
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar F : K^{\triangleright} \to C
                that exhibits a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit+in+a+quasi-category"} colimit co-cone in
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi K
                      %mo ′
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ▹
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mi p
                      %msup
                        %mi K
                        %mo ▹
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mover
                          %mi F
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (K')^\triangleright \stackrel{p}{\to} K^{\triangleright} \stackrel{\bar F}{\to} C
                is a colimit co-cone.
            %p and equivalently precisely if
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  … and for every
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                        %mo :
                        %mi K
                        %mo →
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : K \to C
                  the morphism
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi C
                        %mo →
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi F
                        %mo ∘
                        %mi p
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        F/C \to (F\circ p)/C
                %p
                  of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/over+quasi-category"} under quasi-categories
                  induced by composition with
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
                  is an equivalence of
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-categories
          .proof
            %h6#proof_3 Proof
            %p
              This is
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Topos+Theory"} HTT, prop. 4.1.1.8
          %p
            The following result is the
            = succeed "-categorical" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "." do
              %strong Quillen’s Theorem A
          #Recognition.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              %strong
                (recognition theorem for final
                = succeed "-functors)" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn ∞
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %p
              A morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo :
                    %mi K
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p : K \to C
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sets"} simplicial sets
              with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-category"} quasi-category
              is final precisely if for each object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \in C
              the
              = succeed "-object" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comma+category"} comma
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi p
                    %mo ≔
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi C
                    %msub
                      %mo ×
                      %mi C
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c/p \coloneqq c/C \times_C K
              is weakly
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contractible"} contractible
          %p
            More explicitly, the comma object in question here is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi c
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mi p
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi c
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mi C
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi K
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi p
                        %mtd
                          %mi C
                          %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                            %mrow
                              %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                              %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  c/p &amp;\to&amp; c/C
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  K &amp;\stackrel{p}{\to}&amp; C
                  \\mathrlap{\,.}
                  }
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c/C
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/over+quasi-category"} under quasi-category
            under
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
          .proof
            %h6#proof_4 Proof
            %p
              This is due to
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andre+Joyal"} Andre Joyal
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Topos+Theory"} HTT, prop. 4.1.3.1
          %p
            The following says that up to equivalence, the cofinal maps of simplicial sets are the right
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anodyne+morphisms"} anodyne morphisms
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_3 Proposition
            %p A map of simplicial sets is cofinal precisely if it factors as a right anodyne map followed by a trivial fibration.
          %p
            This is (
            %a{:href => "#Lurie"}> Lurie, cor. 4.1.1.12
            ).
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#General General
          .num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p
              The inclusion
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast \to \mathcal{C}
              of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object+in+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} terminal object
              is final.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_5 Proof
            %p
              By theorem
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#Recognition"}
              the inclusion of the point is final precisely if for all
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \in \mathcal{C}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categorical+hom-space"} (∞,1)-categorical hom-space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi c
                    %mo ,
                    %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}(c,\ast)
              is contractible. This is the definition of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
              being terminal.
          .num_example
            %h6#example_2 Example
            %p
              A (weak) localization
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒟
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: \mathcal{C} \to \mathcal{D}
              is both initial and final.
          %p
            This appears, for example, as (
            %a{:href => "#Cisinski"}> Cisinski, 7.1.10
            ).
          %h3#CofiberProductsInCosliseCategories Cofiber products in co-slice categories
          .num_example
            %h6#example_3 Example
            %p
              Consider the inclusion of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/walking+structure"} walking
              = succeed "-category," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/span"} span
